course: dictionary
lesson: 793
title: One word, many meanings: beef
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the sentence tells you which one. «At dinner Mum says "We're having beef tonight"» is about food. «What's your beef?» is about a quarrel. Look at the words around the word first: they are its context, and they leave clues.
explain: Beef is a noun, and it can also be a verb. «Picture a farm in 1100» gives a history clue. The farmer looks after a cow, but when the meat reaches the lord's table it is called beef, from French «bœuf». The animal kept its English name and the meat got a French one.
explain: Slang and informal meanings are different again. «He has beef with me» means he has a quarrel with me. «To beef up» means to make something stronger or bigger. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
fact: Beef is the meat from a cow, bull or ox.
fact: In slang, «beef» can mean a complaint or a quarrel, as in «What's your beef?»
fact: Informal «beef» can also mean muscle or strength.
fact: «To beef up» means to make something stronger or bigger.
fact: After 1066 French-speaking lords ate the meat, so English has «beef» from French «bœuf», «pork» from «porc», and «mutton» from «mouton».
exercise: At dinner Mum says «We're having beef tonight». What does beef mean here? | *The meat from a cow | A quarrel with someone | Muscle or strength | To make something bigger
exercise: Ella says Ben has beef with her, so they are not talking. What does beef mean here? | *A quarrel or complaint | The meat from a cow | Strong muscles | To make something stronger
exercise: «Picture a farm in 1100»: the farmer keeps a cow, but what is the meat called at the lord's table? | Steak | Cow | Farm | *Beef
test: Coach says, «We need to beef up our defence». What does beef up mean? | Complain about it | *Make it stronger or bigger | Make it weaker and slower | Cover it in beef
test: Which meaning fits «Vegetarians eat no meat or fish»? | *Meat from an animal | To complain | A quarrel | Muscle or strength
exam: Ruby says, «What's your beef?» in a cross voice. What is she asking? | *What your complaint or quarrel is | How strong your muscles are | Which food you like best | Where the cow lives
